    "answer": {
      "short": "In $(-5)^{-2}$, the base is $-5$, giving $\\frac{1}{25}$. In $-5^{-2}$, the minus sign is outside the power, giving $-\\frac{1}{25}$.",
      "worked": "The parentheses make the negative sign part of the base.\n$$\\begin{align*}(-5)^{-2}&=\\frac{1}{(-5)^2}\\\\&=\\frac{1}{25}\\end{align*}$$\nWithout parentheses, the power applies to $5$ and the minus sign remains outside.\n$$\\begin{align*}-5^{-2}&=-\\frac{1}{5^2}\\\\&=-\\frac{1}{25}\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
